# The fruit stall at the ferry town's market, and I like and I do not like.
lesson language-fruit v=1 format=teach grade=2 unit=5 subject=language levels=[easy, medium, hard] {
  title "[[food.i-like-fruit|cap]]"
  goal "Name eight fruits and vegetables in [[language.name]] and say which you like and which you do not."

  look {
    scene 28x14 {
      column all at=canvas(1, 1) space=1 align=start {
        foodstall market foods=["apple", "banana", "strawberry", "lemon"] names=["[[food.apple]]", "[[food.banana]]", "[[food.strawberry]]", "[[food.lemon]]"] costs=["", "", "", ""]
      }
    }
    say """
      The fruit stall in the ferry town has [[food.the-apple]] (the apple), [[food.the-banana]] (the
      banana), [[food.the-strawberry]] (the strawberry) and [[food.the-lemon]] (the lemon). To say
      you like something, say [[phrase.i-like-fruit]], I like fruit. To say you do not, say
      [[phrase.i-do-not-like-lemon]], I do not like lemon.
      """
  }
  do {
    level easy {
      worked language.fruit-name k=4
    }
    show language.fruit-name k=3 easy-k=0 hard-k=6
    show language.fruit-meaning k=3 easy-k=1 hard-k=4
    show language.fruit-write k=3 easy-k=1 hard-k=5
    show language.fruit-name k=5 easy-k=2 hard-k=7
    show language.fruit-match k=2 easy-k=0 hard-k=3
    show language.fruit-write k=4 hard-k=6
    show language.fruit-listen k=3 easy-k=0 hard-k=5
    show language.fruit-say k=1 easy-k=0 hard-k=2
    level hard {
      show language.fruit-write k=3
    }
  }
  language es {
    try stars=2 {
      show language.es-fruit-why k=1 easy-k=0 hard-k=2
    }
  }
  try stars=3 {
    show language.fruit-puzzle k=1 easy-k=0 hard-k=2
  }
  remember {
    say "[[phrase.i-like-fruit]] I like fruit. [[phrase.i-do-not-like-lemon]] I do not like lemon."
  }

  grown-ups """
    The wrong idea to watch for is a dislike heard as a like because the child catches only the
    liking word. The fruit stall draws each fruit the same way and the crates carry no names in the
    questions, so the picture asks the question and the child supplies the word. The written words
    in this lesson are marked without minding accents; the dictation minds them. The listening
    question is a dictation: read the sentence aloud from this sheet, where it prints as Read aloud,
    slowly and twice, or let the device play it. It is marked word by word, and a missing accent
    counts. If the child is stuck, taste two fruits and say for each whether you like it. Off the
    page, make a shopping list of three fruits. It meets England's key stage 2 languages programme
    (years 3 to 6, statutory from age 7): "engage in conversations; ask and answer questions;
    express opinions and respond to those of others"; Japan's grades 3 and 4 foreign language
    activities: "Activities to ask and answer simple questions about each other's likes and wants,
    etc."; and CEFR A1, "Can understand and use familiar everyday expressions and very basic
    phrases". The phrases are in [[language.variety]].
    """
  level easy {
    grown-ups "At this level [[food.the-pear]] is worked first in the teacher's pen, the words to name are [[food.the-apple]] and [[food.the-orange]] and the words to write are [[food.strawberry]] and [[food.tomato]], the plainer ones of the lesson, with the first set to match, the shortest sentence to hear and the first version of each star question."
  }
  level hard {
    grown-ups "At this level the words to name are [[food.the-grapes]] and [[food.the-tomato]] and the words to write are [[food.lemon]], [[food.banana]] and [[food.orange-fruit]], the ones most often mixed up, with the last set to match, the longest sentence to hear, a third word to write and the last version of each star question."
  }
}
